## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming cardinality with bounded memory using probabilistic data structures; beginner-friendly but shows real trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for distinct count estimation
- Hashing, leading-zero counting, registers
- Time: O(1) per item, Memory: O(m)
- Error ~1.04/√m; choose m from m ≈ (1.04/ε)^2

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal HyperLogLog sketch (illustrative)
class HLL {
  constructor(p = 12) {
    this.m = 1 << p;
    this.reg = new Uint8Array(this.m);
  }
  add(item) {
    const h = hash64(item); // 64-bit int hash
    const p = Math.log2(this.m);
    const j = Number(h >>> (64 - p));
    const w = h & ((1n << BigInt(64 - p)) - 1n);
    const rank = 1 + clz64(w);
    if (this.reg[j] < rank) this.reg[j] = rank;
  }
  estimate() {
    // bias-corrected estimator placeholder
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you pick p for a given error? Explain m↔ε relation.
- What tests would you run to validate accuracy on synthetic streams?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic spatial indices under batched inserts and to reason about amortized bounds, practical thresholds, and rebuild strategies in proximity queries common in ML data pipelines or trading systems.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-grid hashing and locality
- Amortized analysis with batched updates
- Rebuild/rehash trade-offs and density handling
- Space-time trade-offs vs brute force

## Code Example
```javascript
class GridIndex {
  constructor(D){ this.D=D; this.s=D/Math.sqrt(2); this.buckets=new Map(); }
  cell(coord){ return Math.floor(coord/this.s); }
  key(x,y){ const cx = this.cell(x); const cy = this.cell(y); return cx+','+cy; }
  insert(p){ const x=p[0], y=p[1];
    for(let i=-1;i<=1;i++) for(let j=-1;j<=1;j++){
      const k=(Math.floor(x/this.s)+i)+','+(Math.floor(y/this.s)+j);
      const bucket=this.buckets.get(k); if(bucket){ for(const q of bucket){ if(Math.hypot(x-q[0],y-q[1])<=this.D) return true; } }
    }
    const kk=Math.floor(x/this.s)+','+Math.floor(y/this.s);
    if(!this.buckets.has(kk)) this.buckets.set(kk, []);
    this.buckets.get(kk).push(p);
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle deletions or moving points?
- What are worst-case scenarios if density spikes in a single bucket, and how would you mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests an engineer's ability to design incremental statistics with tight per-batch updates and constant-time queries, a realistic analytics constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Maintaining a nonlinear statistic (entropy) via a linearizable surrogate S
- Precomputing k log k and handling zero edges cleanly
- Incremental updates using two counters per move

## Code Example
```javascript
// G[k] = k * Math.log(k), with G[0] = 0
function processBatch(batch, freq, G, S, n) {
  for (const {from,to} of batch) {
    const fFrom = freq[from];
    freq[from] = fFrom - 1;
    S.value += G[fFrom - 1] - G[fFrom];
    const fTo = freq[to];
    freq[to] = fTo + 1;
    S.value += G[fTo + 1] - G[fTo];
  }
  return Math.log(n) - S.value / n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if m changes over time or updates include new categories?
- How do you handle numerical stability for very large n and skewed distributions?

